# Deployment Flow

Simple deployment flow for this Networked Open edX repo.

## Tags

- Lab image tag: `dev`
- QA image tag: `qa`
- Production image tags: generated release tag and `latest`

Build and push both images when code can affect LMS, CMS/Studio, authoring MFE, learning MFE, or Open edX apps:

```bash
tutor images build openedx  # edx-platform repo changes
tutor images build mfe      # frontend-app-authoring / frontend-app-learning changes
```

## Open edX Local Setup

Recommended local setup is Tutor development mode.

```bash
# Install Tutor first if it is not installed.
tutor dev launch
tutor dev start -d
```

Useful local Tutor commands:

```bash
tutor dev start -d lms cms
tutor dev stop
tutor dev restart lms
tutor dev restart cms
tutor dev logs -f lms
tutor dev logs -f cms
```

If running this repo directly without Tutor, required basics are Ubuntu, Python 3.11, Node 18, MySQL, Mongo, and Memcached.

```bash
pip install -r requirements/edx/dev.txt
pip install -r requirements/edx/assets.txt
npm clean-install --dev

./manage.py lms migrate
./manage.py lms migrate --database=student_module_history
./manage.py cms migrate

npm run build-dev
```

## Start Local LMS and CMS

Tutor local/dev:

```bash
tutor dev start -d lms cms
```

Direct Django run:

```bash
./manage.py lms runserver 0.0.0.0:18000
./manage.py cms runserver 0.0.0.0:18010
```

Common local URLs:

- LMS: `http://localhost:18000`
- CMS/Studio: `http://localhost:18010`

## VS Code SSH Server Development

Common Networked development flow is to connect directly to the server from VS Code/Cursor using SSH, edit code on the server, and test from the local browser.

1. Connect to the target server with VS Code/Cursor Remote SSH.
2. Open the server-side `edx-platform` repo folder.
3. Make code changes directly on the server branch.
4. Restart the needed Tutor service or pod.
5. Add the server IP/domain mapping in the local system hosts file if needed.
6. Open the mapped local/test domain in Chrome and verify LMS/CMS/Networked flow.

Hosts file idea:

```text
<server-ip> <test-domain>
```

example edx-platform server hosts mapping:

```text
#edx-platform
3.17.142.177 local.openedx.io studio.local.openedx.io apps.local.openedx.io
# End of section
```

Example browser checks:

- LMS/course site: `http://local.openedx.io` or `https://local.openedx.io`
- CMS/Studio site: `http://studio.local.openedx.io` or `https://studio.local.openedx.io`
- Open edX apps/MFE site: `http://apps.local.openedx.io` or `https://apps.local.openedx.io`

Hot reload is available on this server setup. When working through VS Code/Cursor SSH, many code changes can be checked directly in Chrome on the mapped domains after hot reload or a small service restart, without rebuilding Docker images every time.

For quick server-side dev restart:

```bash
tutor dev restart lms
tutor dev restart cms
```

For Kubernetes/server pod refresh:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
kubectl delete pod -n <namespace> <lms-pod>
kubectl delete pod -n <namespace> <cms-pod>
```

This server-SSH flow is useful for fast QA/lab testing because local Chrome can hit the server through the mapped IP/domain while code is edited directly on the running environment.

